"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunkp_vs_np_docs=globalThis.webpackChunkp_vs_np_docs||[]).push([[6564],{31449(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"tag":{"label":"heuristics","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/tags/heuristics","allTagsPath":"/azeon/p-vs-np/tags","count":11,"items":[{"id":"heuristics/approximating-vertex-cover","title":"Approximating Vertex Cover","description":"Back to the cameras from Posts 11 and 16. Pick any unwatched road and put cameras on both ends. Repeat. That\'s the whole algorithm, it\'s never worse than twice the best, and the proof fits in four lines.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/heuristics/approximating-vertex-cover"},{"id":"heuristics/approximation-algorithms","title":"Approximation Algorithms","description":"You can\'t find the best answer quickly, but you can sometimes prove your quick answer is never worse than twice the best, without ever knowing what the best is. Some problems allow amazing guarantees. Others allow none at all.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/heuristics/approximation-algorithms"},{"id":"heuristics/coping-with-np-hardness","title":"Coping with NP-Hardness","description":"So your problem is NP-Hard. Now what? Engineers don\'t give up; they choose what to give up. Exactness, speed on every input, or generality: pick which one to sacrifice, and a whole toolbox opens.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/heuristics/coping-with-np-hardness"},{"id":"heuristics/dynamic-programming-limits","title":"Dynamic Programming Limits","description":"Dynamic programming solves knapsack exactly with a simple table. It looks polynomial. It isn\'t, and watching the table grow one digit at a time shows exactly why pseudo-polynomial time breaks down for big numbers.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/heuristics/dynamic-programming-limits"},{"id":"heuristics/greedy-algorithms","title":"Greedy Algorithms","description":"Take the best-looking option right now, never look back. It\'s the simplest strategy there is. Sometimes it\'s provably perfect, sometimes it\'s badly wrong, and learning to tell which is one of the most useful skills in algorithm design.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/heuristics/greedy-algorithms"},{"id":"heuristics/local-search-algorithms","title":"Local Search Algorithms","description":"Start with any solution, even a terrible random one. Make a small change. Keep it if it helps. Repeat until nothing helps. That\'s local search, it powers a huge amount of real-world optimisation, and its one big weakness is getting stuck.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/heuristics/local-search-algorithms"},{"id":"heuristics/ptas-and-fptas","title":"PTAS and FPTAS","description":"For some NP-Hard problems you get to choose: name any accuracy you like, say within 1% of the best, and there\'s a polynomial-time algorithm that achieves it. The price is time. Knapsack is the classic example, and it uses a rounding trick I love.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/heuristics/ptas-and-fptas"},{"id":"real-world-impact/resource-allocation-in-saas","title":"Resource Allocation in SaaS","description":"Every cloud app runs on servers, and someone has to decide which job goes on which machine. Use too many machines and you waste money; pack too tightly and things crash. Underneath is bin packing, and it\'s NP-Hard.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/real-world-impact/resource-allocation-in-saas"},{"id":"real-world-impact/supply-chain-logistics","title":"Supply Chain Logistics","description":"Every parcel van starts the morning with a Traveling Salesman Problem plus extra rules. Companies can\'t solve it perfectly, so they solve it well, and the gap between \'good\' and \'best\' is measured in millions of kilometres.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/real-world-impact/supply-chain-logistics"},{"id":"heuristics/the-fractional-knapsack","title":"The Fractional Knapsack","description":"Same bag, same items, one rule changed: you may take part of an item. The greedy strategy that failed in Post 21 becomes perfect, and the reason explains why relaxing a hard problem gives you a bound on its answer.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/heuristics/the-fractional-knapsack"},{"id":"heuristics/tsp-and-the-triangle-inequality","title":"TSP and the Triangle Inequality","description":"General TSP can\'t be approximated at all. Add one sensible rule, that going direct is never longer than taking a detour, and a spanning tree gives a tour within twice the best. Christofides\' 1976 trick gets that to 1.5.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/heuristics/tsp-and-the-triangle-inequality"}],"unlisted":false}}')}}]);